# Team handbook

Everything a new **teammate** needs.

## Contents

1. Share a calendar event
2. Rotate an API key

## Share a calendar event

*Invite a colleague to an existing Calendar event.*

This guide shows how to invite someone to an event you **already** created.

### Prerequisites

- A Mac signed in to iCloud
- An existing event

### Open the event

#### 1. Click **Calendar** in the Dock

![Click Calendar in the Dock](images/01-click-calendar-in-the-dock.png)

#### 2. Double-click the *Team sync* event

![Double-click the Team sync event](images/02-double-click-the-team-sync-event.png)

The event opens in its own window.

> **Tip:** You can also select the event and press `⌘O` to open it.

### Invite people

#### 3. Type the guest's email in **Add Invitees**

![Type the guest's email in Add Invitees](images/03-type-the-guest-s-email-in-add-invitees.png)

Separate several guests with commas.

- Guests get an email invite
- You can remove them later

#### 4. Right-click the guest and choose **Send Email**

![Right-click the guest and choose Send Email](images/04-right-click-the-guest-and-choose-send-email.png)

#### 5. Press `⌘S` to save

![Press ⌘S to save](images/05-press-s-to-save.png)

> **Note:** Guests see changes after they [refresh their calendar](https://support.apple.com/guide/calendar/welcome/mac).

Next, the *security* basics.

## Rotate an API key

*Replace a leaked or expiring API key without downtime.*

Rotate keys every *90 days*, or right away if one leaks.

### Prerequisites

Admin access to the console.

> **Warning:** Creating a new key **revokes** the old one immediately. Update these first:
>
> 1. The CI secret
> 2. Local `.env` files

#### 6. Open **Settings → API keys**

![Open Settings → API keys](images/06-open-settings-api-keys.png)

#### 7. Enter your password to confirm

![Enter your password to confirm](images/07-enter-your-password-to-confirm.png)

Password input is masked while recording.

#### 8. Click **Create key** and copy the new key

![Click Create key and copy the new key](images/08-click-create-key-and-copy-the-new-key.png)

The key is shown **once**. Store it in your password manager; see [Managing keys](https://example.com/docs/keys).

> **Decision:** Is the key used in **production**?
>
> - **Yes:** Go to Step 9
> - **No:** Go to Step 10
> - **Unsure:** Go to the guide “Find where an API key is used”

#### 9. Drag the new key to **Production**

![Drag the new key to Production](images/09-drag-the-new-key-to-production.png)

#### 10. Check that the key works

![Check that the key works](images/10-check-that-the-key-works.png)

Run this in Terminal:

`curl -H "x-api-key: $KEY" https://api.example.com/v1/ping`

It should print *pong*.
